      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probabilities of two independent events occurring separately and then use these probabilities to find the combined probability.\n\nThe first event is rolling a number less than three on the first die. The possible outcomes for a number less than three are 1 and 2. Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes on a die. The probability of this event is therefore:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die less than 3}) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe second event is rolling a number greater than three on the second die. The possible outcomes for a number greater than three are 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes on a die. The probability of this event is therefore:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{second die greater than 3}) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince these two events are independent, the combined probability is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{both events}) = P(\\text{first die less than 3}) \\times P(\\text{second die greater than 3})\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the individual probabilities, we get:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{both events}) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 \\times 1}{3 \\times 2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
