      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can break down the problem into individual probabilities and then multiply them together.\n\nFirst, we'll consider the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die. \n\nA six-sided die has six faces, numbered 1 through 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes (rolling a 1 or a 2).\n\nThe probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability}( \\text{Firstdie} < 3 ) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we'll consider the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die.\n\nThe numbers greater than three on a six-sided die are 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes (rolling a 4, 5, or 6).\n\nThe probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability}( \\text{Seconddie} > 3 ) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the combined probability of both events happening simultaneously. Since the two events are independent (the outcome of the first die does not affect the outcome of the second die), we multiply their individual probabilities:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Combined Probability} = \\text{Probability}( \\text{Firstdie} < 3 ) * \\text{Probability}( \\text{Seconddie} > 3 ) = \\frac{1}{3} * \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nPerforming the multiplication gives us:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}",
